entities.description:
Chalteaufite is a character in Final Fantasy XIV. He is the member of the Passage of the Unbound who stole the Steelhog Vessel. Chalteaufite is the antagonist during the Physical Ranged DPS role quests in the Dawntrail expansion.

Chalteaufite was one of Apyaahi's guests who joined her villainous group, the Passage of the Unbound. Apyaahi revealed the existence of sacred artifacts around Tural. These artifacts contain the essence of fearsome beasts called Tural Vidraal, and said that with the power of the artifacts they could achieve their goal..}} Following Apyaahi's instructions, Chalteaufite disguised himself as a merchant and infiltrated a village in the south of Yok Tural. Chalteaufite attracted the attention of the villagers by displaying exotic goods from foreign lands and serving them free drinks. Under cover of night, Chalteaufite stole the Steelhog Vessel and fled back to Ala Mhigo. Following the trail of an empty bottle Chalteaufite left behind, Ceetol Ja was able to follow him to Gyr Abania in an attempt to recover the treasure..|name=To Steal a Steelhog}}

Using the powers of the Steelhog Vessel, Chalteaufite deceived several poor citizens of Ul'dah and made promises of change. Chalteaufite gathered a group of subordinates and forced them to join him in attacks. The followers quickly became disillusioned, although their attempts to protest were met with threats and violence by Chalteaufite. At one point, Chalteaufite recruited a Mamool Ja mercenary called Kaqool Ja. Chalteaufite told Kaqool Ja that the cost of a ship back to Tural was enough to buy a mansion in Eorzea, and said that if the mercenary served him loyally, Chalteaufite would cover all the costs of his journey home..}}

Using the vessel's power, Chalteaufite began attacking trade caravans coming from Gridania, claiming that the merchants were overworking and underpaying their workers and therefore deserved to be plundered..|name=Bandits Abound}} As Chalteaufite's gang attacked a merchant and his escort, the Warrior of Light arrived to help the victims. Chalteaufite used the Steelhog Vessel's power on Kaqool Ja and ordered him to deal with the Warrior of Light. Just as Kaqool Ja drew his axe, Ceetol Ja arrived on the scene. Upon seeing a compatriot, Kaqool Ja refused to fight and put away his weapon. Ceetol Ja asked Chalteaufite to return the treasure, but he refused, saying that only he would know how to harness the Vessel's power. While Chalteaufite fled, Ceetol Ja took the wounded to Rhalgr's Reach.

Ellerete is a non-playable character in Final Fantasy XIV. She and Kokonowa are the members of the Passage of the Unbound who stole the Heartsting. Ellerete is the antagonist during the Healer role quests in the Dawntrail expansion.

Ellerete was one of Apyaahi's guests who joined her villainous group. Apyaahi revealed the existence of sacred artifacts around Tural. These artifacts contain the essence of fearsome beasts called Tural Vidraal, and said that with the power of the artifacts they could achieve their goal..}}

Following Apyaahi's instructions, Ellerete and Kokonowa managed to infiltrate a village in northern Xak Tural and stole the Heartsting, a relic capable of controlling all types of toxins. When Nyipine and Loashkana left the village to recover the treasure, Ellerete and Kokonowa ambushed the two, and a mammet inflicted severe injuries on the Nyipine's foot. Escaping their pursuers, Ellerete and Kokonowa fled to Limsa Lominsa and began using the treasure to cause several incidents of blindness..}}

After successfully deceiving Wyssulm and Zerig into purchasing tainted potions in Aleport, Ellerete found immense amusement in watching Zerig suffer from uncontrollable hiccups due to the toxins. When Loashkana and the Warrior of Light arrived just in time to rescue the adventurers from danger, Ellerete made her dramatic appearance. She gleefully taunted her pursuers, taking pride in her role as an agent of chaos. With her characteristic flair, she introduced herself and Kokonowa, mocking the adventurers for their plight and boasting about her experiments with poisons. After toying with them long enough, she vanished into a cloud of foul smoke..}}

Guildivain Sailmet also known as Faldrinet, is a non-player character in Final Fantasy XIV, and is the main antagonist of the Sage questline.

Faldrinet studied the art of somonautics and became a Sage eventually falling in love with the Viera woman known as Hatia, another Sage from Psysis Technon. Eventually over time his lover became ill from an incurable disease and died within a week. Wracked with grief, Faldrinet fell to madness seeking ways to enhance and cure all illnesses through any means necessary. He used human test subjects in a procedure sought to enhance the body in order to cure them of their illness. Unfortunately, many lost their lives and those that live suffered through the symptoms of the enhancements and the mental trauma of being the only survivors. Rumors abound of his experiments, and he covered up his tracks in Sharlayan before heading off to Eorzea.

Faldrinet traveled to the town of Idyllshire, where he sought to obtain some muscmaloi from Saint Mocianne's Arboretum before he was cornered by Loifa, Ancel, and Mahaud. Before his attackers could do any harm, he dodges one of Loifa's spells, and before Mahaud could hit him with a more potent spell, the Warrior of Light jumps in front of him but is protected by Loifa with Haima. Afterwards Ancel states that there are too many prying eyes and teleports away, threatening them to not get in their way again. They aid the man, learning that his name is Faldrinet from Sharlayan, traveling the realm to heal those in need with the sage art. Lalah questions him to investigate what his attackers motives are and where they planned to head next, learning that they are going to Saint Mocianne's Arboretum for certain herbs. Faldrinet notices the Warrior is a sage not from Sharlayan, and after being told they were chosen by the crystal he is at a loss for words, knowing that the crystal belonged to his former lover. He denies something is amiss and states he will once again travel the realm..|name=Sage's Focus}}

Faldrinet travels to Central Thanalan to meet with an apothecary for the herbs instead, but during his transaction Loifa and the others appear to attack him once more. Before anything happens, Lalah and the Warrior show up once again stepping in. Loifa states how he regrets sparing the adventurers life and Lalah questions why they would do such things against good natured souls. Loifa states that they are just fools believing whatever they're told, and he asks Faldrinet about the weight of his sins. Faldrinet states how he fights against the fate of unfortunate souls dying without reason and he fights against this injustice. Loifa enlarges an Antling, as it approaches to attack Faldrinet, he provokes Ancel, causing Loifa's compatriots to lose control and fall to the ground. Faldrinet lures the Antling away and Lalah stays behind to handle the current situation while the adventurer assists Faldrinet..|name=Sands of Despair}}

Hector, also known by his ring name Brute Bomber, is a character from Final Fantasy XIV, introduced in the expansion Dawntrail. He is the champion of the light-heavyweight division of the Ascension Arcadia Championship.

Hector used the soul of a Bomb King and was known for fighting dirty, causing many fighters to be carried into the infirmary. During the fight with the Warrior of Light, Hector appeared to use illegal substances yet still lost. This victory made the Warrior the new champion of the light-heavyweight division, with Yaana being amazed that a newcomer without a regulator was able become champion of the division.

During the coronation ceremony for the new champion, Hector interrupted the event by invading the stage. He claimed the Warrior's victory was a hoax, since they fought with seven others. Hector insisted they settle it one-on-one right there, and activated his feral soul. Hector charged at the Warrior of Light, but before he could hit them Hector was knocked out by a bolt of lightning of Eutrope..}}

When Yaana and Neyuni arrived with the Warrior of Light, they warned Hector about the dangers of using feral souls. They found Hector surrounded by his kittens and Retsarra paying a visit. Retsarra revealed about their past and left to let Yaana talk to Hector. Neyuni's examination confirmed the worst—his soul already showed signs of psychonekrosis. Though Yaana urged him to retire before the disease worsened, Hector rejected their warnings in anger. Unknown to they, Retsarra had listened to the entire conversation, leaving quietly while Hector struggled to accept the truth..}}

Hegemone is a character in Final Fantasy XIV. She is an Ancient serving as a Keyward in Pandæmonium.

Prior to Elidibus's investigation into Pandæmonium, Hegemone served as a Keyward of Pandæmonium. She secretly believed that Athena was more suited to serve as the facility's director. This belief, along with Hegemone's pride of being a Keyward, made her susceptible to Athena's enthrallment. Under Athena's influence, and after Lahabrea killed Athena, Hegemone secretly orchestrated the release of the dungeon's captive beasts and used the ensuing chaos to free Hephaistos from his imprisonment. Hephaistos enthralled her as well and fused her with a plant-based parasite..|name=Guided by the Past}}

The Warrior of Light encountered Hegemone in Abyssos: The Sixth Circle, where the Keyward attempted to stop them from advancing further. The Warrior of Light incapacitated her while Elidibus and Erichthonios bound her. Erichthonios used Hegemone's defeat and capture as proof they could save the remaining wards of Pandæmonium rather than kill them as Lahabrea insisted..}}

After the chaos of Pandæmonium had subsided, Hegemone was cured of Hephaistos's enthrallment and began helping the clean-up and restoration of normalcy in the facility. When Lahabrea met with her, he noted that with Agdistis's death, Hegemone was the most senior Keyward of Pandæmonium and made her privy to Hephaistos's origins. Despite her denials, Lahabrea identified Hegemone as someone under Athena's enthrallment and the one responsible for creating the chaos in Pandæmonium and releasing Hephaistos in the first place. Lahabrea removed Athena's spell from Hegemone, but did not kill her. Despite Hegemone's betrayal and possible lingering resentment, Lahabrea let the past slide and appointed her as Pandæmonium's new director. Hegemone accepted Lahabrea's offer and promised to restore Pandæmonium to its former glory.

Index is a non-playable character in Final Fantasy XIV. It is a manifestation of the Archive's knowledge and serves as the antagonist of the Occult Crescent storyline.

During the War of the Magi, Archive, a Nym scholar, sought to preserve part of the civilizations of the era within the Occultic Crescent. As the work progressed, Archive resolved to construct the Forked Towers to protect the island. He initially oversaw their maintenance himself. Although he had once made use of the dangerous knowledge sealed within the towers, Archive vowed never to employ those techniques again, choosing instead to preserve them. He decided to give physical form to his wisdom by creating an entity capable of maintaining the Forked Towers after his eventual death.

During the summoning, however, Archive was tempted by the thought that it would be a waste not to make use of such knowledge. Though the hesitation was fleeting, it tainted the summoning and resulted in Index. The summoned entity, coveting the knowledge for itself, placed a seal upon Archive's magic and knowledge and transformed him into a tonberry to prevent him from attempting any intervention. Although Iris had been instructed only to observe, she intervened to save Archive, but could do nothing about her master's condition. Thereafter, Archive remained asleep, sealed within a sarcophagus, while Index assumed control of the Forked Towers. With no one left to oppose him, Index began exploring the possible futures that awaited the civilizations of the Fifth Astral Era in the North Horn..|name=The Observant Servant}}

1500 years later, the Warrior of Light arrived on the island alongside Ketenramm's crew to uncover its mysteries. They discovered the Archive's sarcophagus and awakened it. The expedition led them to explore the archipelago and defeat several of the Index's familiars. At North Horn, they encountered unstable aether that had left the ruins of the civilizations in a state resembling their original fate following the war, a result of the Index's experiments..}}

Kokonowa is a non-playable character in Final Fantasy XIV. He and Ellerete are the members of the Passage of the Unbound who stole the Heartsting. Kokonowa is one of the antagonists during the Healer role quests in the Dawntrail expansion.

Ellerete and Kokonowa were among Apyaahi's guests who joined her villainous group. Although only Ellerete was present at the meeting, one of Kokonowa's mammets was accompanying her during the reunion. Apyaahi revealed the existence of sacred artifacts around Tural. These artifacts contain the essence of fearsome beasts called Tural Vidraal, and said that with the power of the artifacts they could achieve their goal..}}

Following Apyaahi's instructions, Ellerete and Kokonowa managed to infiltrate a village in northern Xak Tural and stole the Heartsting, a relic capable of controlling all types of toxins. When Nyipine and Loashkana left the village to recover the treasure, Ellerete and Kokonowa ambushed the two, and a mammet inflicted severe injuries on the Nyipine's foot. Escaping their pursuers, Ellerete and Kokonowa fled to Limsa Lominsa and began using the treasure to cause several incidents of blindness..}}

After successfully deceiving Wyssulm and Zerig into purchasing tainted potions in Aleport, Ellerete found immense amusement in watching Zerig suffer from uncontrollable hiccups due to the toxins. When Loashkana and the Warrior of Light arrived just in time to rescue the adventurers from danger, Ellerete made her dramatic appearance. She gleefully taunted her pursuers, taking pride in her role as an agent of chaos. With her characteristic flair, she introduced herself and Kokonowa, mocking the adventurers for their plight and boasting about her experiments with poisons. After toying with them long enough, she vanished into a cloud of foul smoke..}}

Leophyne is a non-playable character in Final Fantasy XIV. She is the member of the Passage of the Unbound who stole the Cradle of the Sleepless. Leophyne is the antagonist during the Tank role quests in the Dawntrail expansion.

Leophyne was born of noble blood, but she always despised the traditions of Ishgard and the responsibilities that came with her position. Though a formidable Temple Knight, known for her prowess in combat and skill with mounts, she often defied the norm by spending nights in the gambling halls, until one day she fell asleep on guard duty, allowing her allies to be slaughtered by dragons. For this failure, she was expelled from the Temple Knights and stripped of her title..}} After her expulsion, her whereabouts became unknown and her name becoming synonymous with disgrace among her former comrades..|name=The Narwhal Beckons}}

Leophyne was one of Apyaahi's guests who joined her villainous group, the Passage of the Unbound. Once all those invited to join the organization gathered at Tuliyollal, Apyaahi revealed the existence of sacred treasures around Tural. These artifacts contain the essence of fearsome beasts called Tural Vidraal, and said that with the power of the artifacts they could achieve their goal..}} Following Apyaahi's instructions, Leophyne came to the village of Xak Tural, posing as a scholar interested in the Cradle of the Sleepless. She managed to deceive the villagers and steal the artifact, which allowed its wielder to transfer fatigue to another and induce sleep. After the theft, she fled to Ishgard with the Cradle, intending to use its power for her own purposes.

Back in Ishgard, Leophyne used the Cradle's powers to attempt to tarnish the reputation of the Temple Knights, ridiculing several knights by making them sleep on duty. Leophyne also released several criminals who held a grudge against the Temple Knights to be her henchmen. Eventually Leophyne and her henchmen confronted the Warrior of Light and Tentoawa who were trying to retrieve the Cradle. Tentoawa attempted to confront her but was quickly overwhelmed by the artifact's power and fell into a deep sleep..}}

Samidare is a non-playable character in Final Fantasy XIV. He is the member of the Passage of the Unbound who stole the Dreamweave Cocoon. Samidare is the antagonist during the Melee DPS role quests in the Dawntrail expansion.

Samidare was one of the Doman ninja who fought to defend Doma against the invasion of the Garlean Empire twenty-five years ago, even fighting alongside guardian of Doma, Tamamo Gozen. However, their efforts were in vain, and Doma fell to the Empire's overwhelming force. Soon after the occupation began, Samidare was recruited into the Garlean army and forced to become a spy. He worked to fan the flames of war in distant lands through rumors and manipulation..|name=Who's Who}}

It was only after the prisoner exchange that he finally returned to Doma, but he was a changed man. Samidare could no longer find joy or solace in peace. He sought to destroy peace, claiming that he wanted to prove that "civility is but a facade to hide the savagery of man." Samidare spread lies to try to turn people against each other, and before he could be confronted by his childhood friend Subaru, he fled. Samidare had received a letter from the Passage of the Unbound inviting him to join the organization.

Samidare was one of Apyaahi's guests who joined her villainous group. Apyaahi revealed the existence of sacred artifacts around Tural. These artifacts contain the essence of fearsome beasts called Tural Vidraal, and said that with the power of the artifacts they could achieve their goal..}}

Wawkesa is a character in Final Fantasy XIV. He is the leader of a group of bandits in the outskirts of Kozama'uka.

After Wuk Lamat obtained the keystone in Earthenshire and was on his way to Yok Huy, Wawkesa, disguised, appeared saying that Rediroq had forgotten to give her something important and asked Wuk Lamat to accompany him back to the village. Wuk Lamat then left with him alone. After a long time, Wuk Lamat's companions discovered that she had never returned to Earthenshire..}} With Koana's help in investigating, they discover Wawkesa's true identity and that his gang frequently attacks merchants in the Many Fires area. Koana decided to disguise hiself as a merchant as bait for the bandits..}}

The plan worked, and Koana managed to trick Wawkesa into trying to loot it. Wawkesa quickly realized it was a trap and fled, leaving his henchmen behind. Once Koana interrogated the captured bandits, they confessed to being hired to capture Wuk Lamat, but only Wawkesa knew the details. Koana left the bandits with the Landsguard, and with Thancred's help, they followed Wawkesa's trail. They discovered that Wawkesa had been hired by one of the Hoobigo who accompanied Bakool Ja Ja..}}

Category:Characters in Final Fantasy XIV Category:Hyur Category:Antagonists

Wihuwte is a non-playable character in Final Fantasy XIV. He is the deputy chief of Dustwatch and, using the pseudonym of Vulture, he is also the leader of the bandits called the Daybreakers.

Approximately one year prior, Wihuwte using the pseudonym "the Vulture", challenged the leaders of the Daybreakers—Zekowa, Kwikana, and Liwine—to a duel. He appeared with his henchmen and defeated Zekowa and his companions. Wihuwte seized control of the Daybreakers and transformed them into a band of criminals. At first, the members of the group feared Wihuwte and obeyed his orders without question. When Wihuwte demonstrated that, by leveraging his position as deputy chief of the Dustwatch, he could ensure their crimes went unpunished and allow them to amass wealth, he fully won them over.

Zekowa and his friends eventually concluded that the only way to stop him was to challenge him to a duel. When the day of the duel arrived, Wihuwte appeared with the Dustwatch and ordered the arrest of Kwikana and Liwine on charges of theft, setting their bail at a sum no one could possibly afford..}}

Outraged by the unjust imprisonment of his companions, Zekowa confronted Wihuwte, the deputy chief of the Dustwatch. Wihuwte mocked Zekowa, claiming he had evidence that Zekowa's friends had committed the theft and declaring the investigation closed. As a frustrated Zekowa was about to raise his gun at Wihuwte, he was interrupted by Estinien preventing Wihuwte from having an excuse to arrest Zekowa. Kemakka, the sheriff of the Dustwatch, asked what was happening, Wihuwte merely told Kemakka not to worry and that he would keep an eye on Zekowa..}}

Koh Rabntah is a researcher for the Sons of Saint Coinach in Final Fantasy XIV, tasked with researching and cataloguing the newly resurfaced Crystal Tower. Upon discovery of an orb in the unearthed tower, Koh was possessed by the spirit of the Allagan archmagus, Noah , known for her treatises on the cosmos and her oneiromancy. Noah was the court-appointed instructor for Unei and Doga, members of the royal bloodline, and transmitted her soul into an orb that survived for three astral and umbral eras until being discovered by Koh.

Koh's colleague, Syele, entrusted the Warrior of Light with finding Koh after she abandoned Saint Coinach's Find in Mor Dhona for Revenant's Toll. The Warrior of Light discovered that Koh had been possessed by the soul of Noah, who had long in the past transmitted her soul into an orb.|name = hardly}} Noah, discovering the Warrior of Light as being part of the Nominated Observers of Artifacts Historical, offered to use their oneiromancy to ascertain the group's discoveries within the Crystal Tower, creating oneiromatic pearls that would contain the memories of the Warrior of Light's expeditions through the tower and into the void. Noah promised that if she found their discoveries worthy, she would impart some manner of knowledge unto the group. Upon reviewing the Warrior of Light's memories, Noah deemed it necessary to partially inform Koh's colleagues about her presence in Koh's body.|name = archmagus}}

Following these events, Noah returned Koh control of her body, though Noah often took the body out for nights of debauchery, to Koh's chagrin.
